	abstract = {Traditional walking canes have offered limited mobility to elderly individuals, while leaving critical safety gaps in spatial awareness and emergency response. This paper presents a novel integrated assistive system that combines obstacle detection, fall detection, biometric monitoring, and sustainable energy harvesting in a single low-cost device, helping to minimize falls in seniors without compromising comfort. The system utilizes ultrasonic sensors with haptic feedback to give the user ample time to respond to obstacles and uneven surfaces with an accuracy of 97.75% ± 1.2% (agreement with a meter-stick baseline) up to 1.5 meters and 100% detection across 27 simulated fall trials. Alongside the hardware, the companion mobile application enables real-time pulse monitoring for the user and comprehensive emergency response capabilities for caregivers. If a fall is detected by the built-in gyroscope, the location is automatically recorded in the mobile app, and an emergency call is placed to emergency services or a family member. A thermoelectric energy harvesting system converts body heat from the user’s palm into electrical energy, sustainably powering two LEDs controlled by a switch. Comprehensive testing validates the effective delivery of haptic feedback and the reliable performance of sensors with a user-adjustable detection range. Results are preliminary from bench/lab simulations; future development includes expanded user testing and baseline comparisons. ©2025 IEEE.},
