	abstract = {Accurate classification of walking surfaces is critical for wearable biomedical and health informatics systems, particularly in applications such as gait analysis, fall prevention, and prosthetic control. However, a systematic evaluation of how different sensing modalities and their feature representations contribute to this measurement task remains lacking. In this study, we propose a multisensor evaluation framework to quantify the effectiveness of diverse signal features and sensor fusions for surface classification. We conduct biomechanical tests on healthy individuals walking naturally across four terrains: concrete road, synthetic track, grass, and sand. After performing signal processing, feature extraction, feature-level data fusion, and feature selection, we employ a random forest (RF) classifier to identify the surfaces. We thoroughly examine the effects of various sensor and training configurations on classification performance. Experimental results show that meaningful feature extraction and selection can enable accurate surface detection with a minimal feature set. In addition, sensor fusion combined with feature selection can enhance performance, with the exception of the combination of insole and inertial sensors. Key features from different sensor configurations are identified, achieving a classification accuracy of 95.2% 3:3% with inertial sensors alone and 97.8% 2:4% with the combination of all sensors. © 2001-2012 IEEE.},
