	doi = {10.1109/CNC68716.2025.11484537},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038027121?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Visually impaired individuals experience significant amounts of challenge in the domains of mobility, autonomy, and personal protection. Conventional assistive devices such as the white cane and guide dogs offer limited coverage and remain insufficient in capability. In this work, we propose the design, development, and testing of a smart wearable assistive band driven by the ESP32 microcontroller that can offer increased access and safety to the visually impaired user. In our considered system, we have integrated multiple sensor inputs, such as a GPS sensor to provide the live location, MPU6050 sensor as a fall detector, and HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor as an obstacle detector. An emergency communication function is activated by the presence of a GSM module, and multimodal output through the use of auditory and vibration alarms is employed to offer the user imminent awareness of the presence of danger close by. Experimental validation confirms that the system can detect emergencies, falls, and obstacles accurately with low energy consumption, thereby offering a reliable, efficient, and affordable assistive solution for visually challenged individuals.  © 2025 IEEE.},
