	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046408770?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Against the accelerating population aging, the elderly and physically impaired groups suffer from difficulties in daily care, safety monitoring and auxiliary activities. This paper proposes a non-invasive BCI-driven assistive robot system integrated with multimodal perception and lightweight neural network reasoning to implement low-cost closed-loop human-robot assistance. Adopting motor imagery electroencephalography (MI-EEG) as core control signal, the system fuses RGB-D visual information and environmental semantic features to construct stable user intention identification and environment perception models. The system forms a complete closed-loop framework covering EEG signal decoding, 20 kg payload manipulator control, fall monitoring, emotion identification, interactive companionship and XR remote manipulation. With network pruning and INT8 quantization optimization, the lightweight model accomplishes real-time inference within 30 ms on embedded edge devices. Experimental results show that the system reaches 90.8%-95.2% MI-EEG recognition accuracy under different interference conditions, with an overall task completion rate of 92% and a fall detection recall of 95%, which meets the real-time and reliability requirements of household scenarios. This study validates the practical engineering feasibility of non-invasive BCI applied to the auxiliary service for the elderly and disabled, and provides technical support for the industrialization of related intelligent assistive equipment. © 2026 IEEE.},
