	abstract = {Assistive mobility technologies play a pivotal role in boosting the independence and overall well-being of people with physical disabilities. Nevertheless, the majority of smart wheelchairs available today operate based on pre-set navigation routes and are not adaptable to changing indoor environments. This research presents a smart wheelchair navigation system that adapts to the environment using a digital twin-based robot wheelchair optimized by Reinforcement Learning (RL) in NVIDIA Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab. The digital twin is created through URDF and USD models of the wheelchair and the indoor environment, thereby allowing a realistic physics simulation. A multimodal sensor suite, which includes a 2D LiDAR, four IMU sensors attached to the wheels, and four directional RGB cameras, is installed to get complete spatial and motion perception. Sensor data are then encoded and merged into a single state representation, which is the input for the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) agent to learn the task of autonomous navigation. Safety measures in real-time, such as fall detection, collision detection, and emergency stop mechanisms, among other things, are what keep the robot safe during training and navigation. The outcome of the simulation shows increased reward progression and navigation success, thus demonstrating the effectiveness of RL-driven digital twins in creating intelligent, user-friendly assistive mobility systems.  © 2026 IEEE.},
