	abstract = {With the global elderly population rising rapidly, complex challenges continue to emerge across geriatric healthcare, personal risk management, and psycho-emotional wellbeing. Issues such as social isolation, reduced mobility, and delayed help during emergencies significantly worsen health outcomes for this at-risk group. The Guided Intelligent Health Observer (GIHO), a cognitive and sensor-integrated companion robot, is designed to address these concerns by offering continuous multi-modal monitoring, intelligent interventions, and engaging user interaction. GIHO employs a distributed sensor network, coordinated by an Arduino Uno, enabling uninterrupted observation of both environmental and physiological parameters. Temperature and humidity readings are captured using DHT11 sensors, vital cardiopulmonary metrics using MAX30100 modules, and movement or accidental fall incidents are detected through the MPU6050 inertial measurement unit. Integrated gas sensors help prevent hazardous exposure. Using an ESP8266 WiFi module, the system securely transmits sensor data to IoT servers for real-time caregiver access. During critical events, the ESP32-CAM captures essential visual evidence and sends it to caregivers via Telegram alerts. A local buzzer and tactile switch system ensures quick alarm escalation, supported by reminders for medication and hydration. The combined use of IoT interoperability, sensor-fusion intelligence, and interactive algorithms enables proactive monitoring, early-warning alerts, and emotional support, resulting in a modular, reliable, and userfriendly solution that improves independence, emotional health, and safety for elderly users. © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0}
}

