	doi = {10.1109/IWCMC69287.2026.11580093},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044708554?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Although smart walkers have evolved in recent years, limitations in energy efficiency and reliable safety persist, highlighting the need for an intelligent and energy-conscious smart walker for elderly and rehabilitation users. This paper presents E-Walk, an energy-efficient smart ambulatory assistance device that provides assisted mobility with user-intent-based motorization, automatic braking triggered by real-time near-fall detection, as well as an obstacle detection module with user attention detection and an alerting system. The user-intention-based drive assistance module uses force sensors to detect movement and turning intention. The waist-mounted Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) streams motion data to a lightweight Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) model for real-time near-fall classification. Moreover, a wireless emergency braking command activates a linear actuator with a rubber pad for braking. The obstacle detection module scans the environment using an ultrasonic sensor and selectively activates a camera for user-attention checking only when obstacles are detected, significantly reducing power consumption. The E-Walk efficiently powers the entire system using a rechargeable battery, regenerative braking, and optimized power distribution to enable over two hours of continuous operation. Experimental testing using real user data proves 98.7% accuracy and 97% precision in near-fall detection, user-intent-based steering across surfaces, and reliable emergency braking response. © 2026 IEEE.},
