	doi = {10.1007/978-3-031-44981-9_21},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85192190199?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Human transports in hospitals are labor-intensive and primarily performed in beds to save time. This transfer method does not promote the mobility or autonomy of the patient. To relieve the caregivers from this time-consuming task, a mobile robot is developed to autonomously transport humans around the hospital. It provides different transfer modes including walking and sitting in a wheelchair. The problem that this paper focuses on is to detect emergencies and ensure the well-being of the patient during the transport. For this purpose, the patient is tracked and monitored with a camera system. OpenPose is used for Human Pose Estimation and a trained classifier for emergency detection. We collected and published a dataset of 18,000 images in lab and hospital environments. It differs from related work because we have a moving robot with different transfer modes in a highly dynamic environment with multiple people in the scene using only RGB-D data. To improve the critical recall metric, we apply threshold moving and a time delay. We compare different models with an AutoML approach. This paper shows that emergencies while walking are best detected by a SVM with a recall of 95.8 % on single frames. In the case of sitting transport, the best model achieves a recall of 62.2 %. The contribution is to establish a baseline on this new dataset and to provide a proof of concept for the human emergency detection in this use case. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2024.},
