	doi = {10.1007/978-3-031-37848-5_31},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85172026799?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Loss of balance in humans is a common cause of falling. Wearable robotic devices for balance support constitute an appealing solution due to the freedom of movement they offer to the users. Researchers have emphasized the advantages of gyroscopes as portable devices to assist human balance during walking. The usual approach to implement these systems generally consists of two steps. A design phase relying on simulations of simplified models and the experimental one. It is challenging to obtain detailed information from isolated systems during experimental approaches, and constitutes a limitation for generating new insights about human-robot interaction phenomena. In this sense, predictive simulations of integrated robotic and neuro-musculoskeletal multi-body systems can reveal many underlying principles involved, motivating the development and improvement of these methods. In this work, we have implemented an OpenSim model of an integrated neuro-musculoskeletal system driven by 18 muscles and a pyramidal cluster of variable speed gyroscopes for balancing the torso in 3D space. We demonstrate that it is possible to predict by simulations how a gyroscopic device can balance the torso of a walking human model driven by muscles as well as its influence on the gait patterns and muscles activity. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.},
